1
00:00:06,453 --> 00:00:08,886
This moment ceases as it arises.

2
00:00:08,966 --> 00:00:11,595
Does it arise, or does it cease?

3
00:00:12,409 --> 00:00:15,359
A koan is not a riddle
with a clever answer.

4
00:00:15,439 --> 00:00:19,965
It is a question built to catch a
habit in the act. So read it slowly.

5
00:00:20,045 --> 00:00:25,304
Before we even try to answer, the question
has already given us something. An "it".

6
00:00:25,384 --> 00:00:28,769
A moment that must either arise, or cease.

7
00:00:28,849 --> 00:00:32,863
Whatever we do with that "it"
shows which logic we are using.

8
00:00:33,677 --> 00:00:37,609
This is the koan page of The
Logic, from After Anatta:

9
00:00:37,753 --> 00:00:40,852
notes by John Tan, known as Thusness.

10
00:00:40,932 --> 00:00:43,017
It works through the koan twice.

11
00:00:43,106 --> 00:00:45,281
First with the logic of substance.

12
00:00:45,361 --> 00:00:47,551
Then with the logic of dependency.

13
00:00:47,631 --> 00:00:51,848
And then it runs the koan through
Nāgārjuna's five core logics.

14
00:00:52,665 --> 00:00:56,280
Start with a sound. A
bell, or a car going by.

15
00:00:56,360 --> 00:00:58,875
At no point is there a sound that stays.

16
00:00:58,955 --> 00:01:01,729
The sound is already passing as it sounds.

17
00:01:01,809 --> 00:01:04,650
Look closely at any moment of experience,

18
00:01:04,730 --> 00:01:06,308
and this is what is found.

19
00:01:06,388 --> 00:01:08,894
The arising is already the ceasing.

20
00:01:09,023 --> 00:01:13,318
"This moment ceases as it arises"
is not a puzzle someone made up.

21
00:01:13,398 --> 00:01:17,059
It is what experience shows,
when it is looked at closely.

22
00:01:17,869 --> 00:01:19,790
The ordinary picture is different.

23
00:01:19,893 --> 00:01:23,522
Things arise, stay for
a while, and then cease.

24
00:01:23,602 --> 00:01:26,321
The early Buddhist
schools made this exact.

25
00:01:26,401 --> 00:01:30,031
Every conditioned thing
has three marks: arising,

26
00:01:30,111 --> 00:01:31,946
abiding, and ceasing.

27
00:01:32,026 --> 00:01:33,753
The picture needs the middle one.

28
00:01:33,833 --> 00:01:36,960
Only something that abides,
even for an instant,

29
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:41,512
can be the "it" that first
arises and later ceases.

30
00:01:41,592 --> 00:01:44,487
The koan quietly removes the abiding.

31
00:01:44,567 --> 00:01:47,562
And then asks its question
about the "it" anyway.

32
00:01:48,377 --> 00:01:50,865
The logic of substance starts with things,

33
00:01:50,945 --> 00:01:52,740
and asks what happens to them.

34
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:55,511
So it starts with "this
moment" as a thing.

35
00:01:55,591 --> 00:01:58,840
Arising and ceasing are
events that befall it.

36
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:02,631
This is the grammar of the
sentence, subject then verb,

37
00:02:02,711 --> 00:02:07,767
taken for the shape of the world.
The moment arises. The moment ceases.

38
00:02:07,847 --> 00:02:11,644
As if there were a moment apart
from the arising and the ceasing.

39
00:02:12,453 --> 00:02:15,443
Given a thing, the
question must be answered.

40
00:02:15,523 --> 00:02:18,926
There are four possible
answers. Each one fails.

41
00:02:19,729 --> 00:02:23,439
It arises. Then it comes
into being, and is there.

42
00:02:23,519 --> 00:02:25,681
But it ceases as it arises.

43
00:02:25,761 --> 00:02:27,819
So it is never there to have arisen.

44
00:02:28,637 --> 00:02:31,519
It ceases. Then something passes away.

45
00:02:31,599 --> 00:02:34,426
But what passes away, if it never stood?

46
00:02:34,506 --> 00:02:39,155
Nāgārjuna says that ceasing makes
no sense for a thing that abides,

47
00:02:39,235 --> 00:02:41,822
and no sense for a thing that does not.

48
00:02:42,660 --> 00:02:47,852
Both. Then one thing comes into being
and passes away at the same time.

49
00:02:47,932 --> 00:02:51,045
But these are opposites,
like light and dark.

50
00:02:51,125 --> 00:02:53,848
They cannot belong to
one thing at one time.

51
00:02:54,661 --> 00:02:58,888
Neither. Then there is no moment
at all, and nothing happens.

52
00:02:58,968 --> 00:03:01,555
Yet a sound is being heard, right now.

53
00:03:02,396 --> 00:03:07,194
For the logic of substance, this is
a paradox. So it looks for a way out.

54
00:03:07,274 --> 00:03:10,468
A timeless Now, in which the moments pass.

55
00:03:10,548 --> 00:03:13,756
An awareness behind the
moments, which stays.

56
00:03:13,836 --> 00:03:17,927
A pure flow, where there
are no things, only process.

57
00:03:18,007 --> 00:03:21,025
Tiny point-instants that
last no time at all.

58
00:03:21,105 --> 00:03:25,269
Or the last resort: nothing
happens, so nothing is real.

59
00:03:26,085 --> 00:03:29,442
All five are the same
logic, one step further on.

60
00:03:29,522 --> 00:03:32,730
Each keeps the "it", and
moves it somewhere else.

61
00:03:32,810 --> 00:03:35,706
The Now becomes the
moment that never ends.

62
00:03:35,786 --> 00:03:38,382
The awareness becomes the Witness again.

63
00:03:38,462 --> 00:03:41,181
The flow becomes the thing
that is really there.

64
00:03:41,261 --> 00:03:44,487
They differ only in what
they say happens to the "it".

65
00:03:45,313 --> 00:03:48,670
The logic of dependency
starts one step earlier.

66
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:51,089
It does not ask what
happens to the moment.

67
00:03:51,169 --> 00:03:53,983
It asks how "this moment"
is picked out at all.

68
00:03:54,063 --> 00:03:58,521
A moment is designated in dependence
on its arising and its ceasing.

69
00:03:58,601 --> 00:04:01,809
Arising is designated in
dependence on ceasing.

70
00:04:01,889 --> 00:04:06,034
And ceasing, in dependence
on arising. None comes first.

71
00:04:06,114 --> 00:04:08,321
None is found apart from the others.

72
00:04:09,125 --> 00:04:14,221
Then "ceases as it arises" is not a
paradox. It is a plain description.

73
00:04:14,301 --> 00:04:19,833
There is no "it" standing between the
arising and the ceasing, to do either.

74
00:04:19,913 --> 00:04:21,816
The question assumes that "it".

75
00:04:21,896 --> 00:04:25,145
When the "it" is looked for and not found,

76
00:04:25,225 --> 00:04:28,284
the question is not
answered. It falls away.

77
00:04:28,364 --> 00:04:30,150
The koan has done its work.

78
00:04:30,969 --> 00:04:35,454
And nothing is lost. Sounds are
still heard. Clocks still keep time.

79
00:04:35,534 --> 00:04:37,288
The firewood still burns.

80
00:04:37,368 --> 00:04:42,669
Just as the flashing is the lightning,
the arising and ceasing is the moment.

81
00:04:42,749 --> 00:04:45,427
There is change, vivid and exact.

82
00:04:45,507 --> 00:04:47,157
And no changer behind it.

83
00:04:47,965 --> 00:04:52,803
Nāgārjuna has five core ways of
taking the logic of substance apart.

84
00:04:52,883 --> 00:04:55,072
Each one can be run on this koan.

85
00:04:55,152 --> 00:04:59,746
Each time, the substance reading needs
a moment that is there on its own,

86
00:04:59,826 --> 00:05:05,439
and fails. Each time, the dependency
reading lets the same moment appear,

87
00:05:05,519 --> 00:05:07,400
with nothing found behind it.

88
00:05:08,214 --> 00:05:10,213
The first is the four corners.

89
00:05:10,293 --> 00:05:14,167
If this moment really arises,
from what? From itself?

90
00:05:14,247 --> 00:05:17,065
Then it was already
there, and nothing arose.

91
00:05:17,154 --> 00:05:19,235
From another, the moment before?

92
00:05:19,315 --> 00:05:23,963
Then two separate things, and nothing
explains how one becomes the other.

93
00:05:24,043 --> 00:05:27,822
From both? That has both
faults. From no cause?

94
00:05:27,902 --> 00:05:29,860
Then moments would appear at random.

95
00:05:29,940 --> 00:05:34,479
Seen with dependency, the moment
is not a thing that needs a source.

96
00:05:34,559 --> 00:05:38,569
It is picked out with its conditions,
the moment before among them.

97
00:05:38,649 --> 00:05:42,949
Nothing passes from one moment to
the next. And nothing is missing.

98
00:05:43,786 --> 00:05:45,989
The second is same or different.

99
00:05:46,069 --> 00:05:48,693
Is the arising the same as the ceasing?

100
00:05:48,773 --> 00:05:54,698
Then to arise is already to cease, and
nothing ever comes to be. Is it different?

101
00:05:54,778 --> 00:05:59,549
Then they are two events, and something
must stand between them to be the moment.

102
00:05:59,629 --> 00:06:01,777
The abiding the koan removed.

103
00:06:01,857 --> 00:06:04,793
Nāgārjuna asks this of fire and fuel.

104
00:06:04,873 --> 00:06:09,019
If the fire were the fuel, the
burner and the burned would be one.

105
00:06:09,099 --> 00:06:13,367
If the fire were other than the fuel,
it could burn with no fuel at all.

106
00:06:13,447 --> 00:06:18,856
Seen with dependency, arising and
ceasing are not one, and not two.

107
00:06:18,936 --> 00:06:22,352
They are designated
together, like fire and fuel.

108
00:06:22,482 --> 00:06:25,654
"Ceases as it arises" says exactly this.

109
00:06:26,486 --> 00:06:29,993
The third is the three
times. Look for the arising.

110
00:06:30,073 --> 00:06:33,607
What has already arisen is
not arising. It is done.

111
00:06:33,687 --> 00:06:37,425
What has not yet arisen is
not arising. It is not here.

112
00:06:37,505 --> 00:06:41,352
And apart from these two,
no "now arising" is found.

113
00:06:41,432 --> 00:06:45,333
Nāgārjuna makes this search
with a road being walked.

114
00:06:45,413 --> 00:06:50,143
The same search, run on
ceasing, finds no "now ceasing".

115
00:06:50,223 --> 00:06:54,042
The koan's "as it arises"
has no room to happen in.

116
00:06:54,122 --> 00:06:59,681
Seen with dependency, arisen and not yet
arisen are designated in relation to each

117
00:06:59,761 --> 00:07:04,966
other. No present stands between
past and future, holding the moment.

118
00:07:05,046 --> 00:07:09,767
The moment is met, vividly,
without a now that contains it.

119
00:07:10,602 --> 00:07:12,818
The fourth is infinite regress.

120
00:07:12,898 --> 00:07:17,438
If the arising of this moment is
a real event, it is conditioned.

121
00:07:17,518 --> 00:07:21,405
Then it needs its own
arising, abiding and ceasing.

122
00:07:21,485 --> 00:07:23,824
And those need theirs, without end.

123
00:07:23,904 --> 00:07:29,354
Ask "does it arise?", and you must
also ask "does the arising arise?",

124
00:07:29,434 --> 00:07:31,338
and so on, for ever.

125
00:07:31,417 --> 00:07:36,066
Seen with dependency, arising is
not a thing that needs to arise.

126
00:07:36,146 --> 00:07:39,897
It is a designation, made in
dependence on what it marks.

127
00:07:39,977 --> 00:07:43,253
There is no regress, and no first arising.

128
00:07:43,333 --> 00:07:48,267
In Nāgārjuna's words, what comes
to be in dependence is at peace.

129
00:07:48,347 --> 00:07:50,323
And so is arising itself.

130
00:07:51,142 --> 00:07:53,291
The fifth is mutual dependence.

131
00:07:53,371 --> 00:07:56,538
The four logics above
all fail for one reason.

132
00:07:56,618 --> 00:08:00,315
Each needs a moment, an
arising, or a ceasing,

133
00:08:00,395 --> 00:08:04,133
that is established on its own
before it relates to anything.

134
00:08:04,213 --> 00:08:07,937
Arising and ceasing cannot
be established together,

135
00:08:08,017 --> 00:08:10,220
and cannot be established apart.

136
00:08:10,300 --> 00:08:12,435
So how could they be established at all?

137
00:08:12,515 --> 00:08:15,560
This fifth logic is not
one more refutation.

138
00:08:15,640 --> 00:08:17,761
It is what the other four leave behind.

139
00:08:17,841 --> 00:08:20,967
Dependent arising is
what we call emptiness.

140
00:08:21,047 --> 00:08:25,573
And because this moment is empty,
it can appear and pass at all.

141
00:08:25,653 --> 00:08:29,799
As Nāgārjuna puts it: for
whom emptiness is possible,

142
00:08:29,879 --> 00:08:31,488
everything is possible.

143
00:08:32,293 --> 00:08:36,724
Each logic searches a different
hiding place of the "it". Its source.

144
00:08:36,804 --> 00:08:40,025
Its identity. Its present. Its marks.

145
00:08:40,105 --> 00:08:41,778
And its standing on its own.

146
00:08:41,858 --> 00:08:45,338
Each lands in the same
place. No "it" is found.

147
00:08:45,418 --> 00:08:47,095
And the moment is still here.

148
00:08:47,921 --> 00:08:50,286
So, does it arise, or cease?

149
00:08:50,366 --> 00:08:53,769
Two answers can be given,
and they are not in conflict.

150
00:08:54,586 --> 00:08:57,686
Conventionally, it arises and ceases.

151
00:08:57,766 --> 00:08:59,778
The sound starts and ends.

152
00:08:59,858 --> 00:09:03,895
We can say correctly when the bell was
struck, and when the ringing stopped.

153
00:09:03,975 --> 00:09:06,359
Nothing in the analysis touches this.

154
00:09:07,177 --> 00:09:11,743
Looked for, nothing that arises
and nothing that ceases is found.

155
00:09:11,823 --> 00:09:14,964
This is how Nāgārjuna
opens his whole work.

156
00:09:15,044 --> 00:09:18,360
He praises the Buddha who
taught dependent arising:

157
00:09:18,440 --> 00:09:22,654
without ceasing, without
arising, without annihilation,

158
00:09:22,734 --> 00:09:26,567
without permanence, not
one thing and not many,

159
00:09:26,647 --> 00:09:28,976
without coming and without going.

160
00:09:29,897 --> 00:09:33,527
"Without arising" is not a
denial that things appear.

161
00:09:33,607 --> 00:09:36,366
It is said of dependent arising itself.

162
00:09:36,446 --> 00:09:40,293
Because nothing arises as
an "it", from its own side,

163
00:09:40,373 --> 00:09:45,157
things can appear, change and
pass at all. Not two worlds.

164
00:09:45,237 --> 00:09:47,390
One moment, seen two ways.

165
00:09:48,201 --> 00:09:51,259
So the koan is not answered
by choosing a corner.

166
00:09:51,339 --> 00:09:55,145
It is answered when the "it" is
seen never to have been there.

167
00:09:55,225 --> 00:09:59,683
Then each moment is simply complete
where it is. Nothing carried over.

168
00:09:59,763 --> 00:10:03,909
Nothing left behind. Dōgen
says it of firewood and ash.

169
00:10:03,989 --> 00:10:08,202
Each abides in its own place,
with a before and an after,

170
00:10:08,282 --> 00:10:10,757
yet cut off from before and after.

171
00:10:10,837 --> 00:10:13,746
The Dzogchen tradition
calls it self-release.

172
00:10:13,826 --> 00:10:19,235
A thought, recognised as unfixed,
releases itself as it arises.

173
00:10:19,315 --> 00:10:22,541
Like a drawing on water,
which needs no eraser.

174
00:10:23,378 --> 00:10:26,070
The koan holds three insights at once.

175
00:10:26,150 --> 00:10:29,086
The moment is vivid, and unfindable.

176
00:10:29,166 --> 00:10:31,844
It appears whenever
conditions come together:

177
00:10:31,924 --> 00:10:35,309
bell, air, ear and attention.

178
00:10:35,389 --> 00:10:38,937
It comes from no hidden
place, and goes into none.

179
00:10:39,017 --> 00:10:43,869
And the "it" that must either arise
or cease is the habit at work.

180
00:10:43,949 --> 00:10:48,665
We habitually think inherently, and the
grammar of the question carries the habit

181
00:10:48,745 --> 00:10:49,945
in.

182
00:10:50,201 --> 00:10:51,425
Why does this matter?

183
00:10:51,505 --> 00:10:57,227
If the moment is an "it", every experience
is something to keep, or to lose.

184
00:10:57,307 --> 00:11:00,786
A clear moment in meditation
must be held on to.

185
00:11:00,866 --> 00:11:03,286
A painful one must be got rid of.

186
00:11:03,366 --> 00:11:06,928
The end of a life is the end of
something that was really there.

187
00:11:07,008 --> 00:11:12,027
Craving and fear follow from the
"it", as surely as the paradox does.

188
00:11:12,837 --> 00:11:17,376
Seen without the "it", there is
nothing to keep and nothing to lose.

189
00:11:17,456 --> 00:11:20,895
Each moment is wholly
itself, and wholly gone,

190
00:11:20,975 --> 00:11:24,319
and nothing went anywhere.
This is not indifference.

191
00:11:24,399 --> 00:11:28,549
The moment is met fully, because
it is not being held on to.

192
00:11:29,366 --> 00:11:35,366
So, wherever you are, listen to a single
sound until it ends. When did it arise?

193
00:11:35,562 --> 00:11:40,459
When did it cease? Look for a sound
between the two, apart from the sounding.

194
00:11:41,261 --> 00:11:44,727
Ask what arises, and
the koan is a paradox.

195
00:11:44,807 --> 00:11:49,759
See that no "it" is found, and
the koan is simply this moment.

